**Enhancement**

The list currently uses the standard CSS variables that are shared across multiple components, it would be useful if the list had specific variables that use the current variables by default that could be overridden in a variant in order to independently customise styles for behaviours such as selected and hover. For example (using Material, would require complimentary vars in other theme variants):

```css
--mdc-list-item-selected-background: var(--mdc-theme-on-surface-hover);
--mdc-list-item-selected-text: var(--mdc-text-color);
--mdc-list-item-hover-background: var(--mdc-theme-on-surface);
```

This improvement suggestion is a clone from DeeMoney iOS project: https://github.com/dee-money/passenger-ios/issues/210

## Why

Normally, when we want to execute a block of code on main thread, what we would usually do is putting that block of code inside `DispatchQueue.main.async`. This way works just fine, especially in the case the code is currently running on background thread. 

However, in the situation when the block of code is already running on main thread, this action will cause the block of code to be rescheduled to run on main thread in the next runloop instead of the current one, causing a small delay in execution. An example symptom we usually see can be if the code is about showing some subviews, there will be a slight delay then a sudden pop to show the view.

## Further comment, evidence, alternatives considered

As a result, for solving this problem, we can create a `Thread+Async.swift` extension file for adding a current thread check before executing as follow:
```
extension Thread {

    static func performOnMain(_ execution: @escaping EmptyCompletion) {
        guard !Thread.isMainThread else { return execution() }
        DispatchQueue.main.async { execution() }
    }
}
```

Then, whenever we want to bring some code to main thread, instead of putting it inside `DispatchQueue.main.async`, we can do this 🤗
```
Thread.performOnMain {
   ...
   // execute your code on main thread here
   ...
}
```

Simple, low-impact change that will should help people avoid capturing stale state in inline lambdas. Because this is undisoverable:

```kotlin
return when (state) {
  is SelectingAge -> 
    Rendering(
      onClick = eventHandler { newAge ->
        this.state = this.state.copy(age = newAge)
      }
    )
  //...
```

And this is at least a bit less so:

```kotlin
return when (renderedState) {
  is SelectingAge -> 
    Rendering(
      onClick = eventHandler { newAge ->
       state = state.copy(age = newAge)
      }
    )
  //...
```

